In Coding 5 you built figures with **ggplot2**. Today you will **reshape tables** with dplyr and tidyr: filter rows with conditions, add columns, chain steps with pipes, and join metadata. The two tables below are a reference for the whole activity and for your assignments.

The first time you click **Run Code**, your browser downloads R once (a few seconds). After that it is quick.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## dplyr and tidyr functions you will use most (click to expand)

| Function | What it does | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `glimpse()` | Shows rows, columns, and column types | `glimpse(penguins)` |
| `filter()` | Keeps rows that meet a condition | `filter(penguins, species == "Adelie")` |
| `select()` | Keeps only the columns you name | `select(penguins, species, body_mass_g)` |
| `mutate()` | Adds a new column computed from existing ones | `mutate(penguins, body_mass_kg = body_mass_g / 1000)` |
| `arrange()` | Sorts rows by a column; wrap in `desc()` for largest first | `arrange(penguins, desc(body_mass_g))` |
| `summarise()` | Collapses many rows into one summary number | `summarise(penguins, avg = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))` |
| `group_by()` | Makes the next summary happen once per group | <code>penguins &#124;&gt; group_by(species) &#124;&gt; summarise(n = n())</code> |
| `count()` | Counts rows per group in one step | `count(penguins, species)` |
| `pull()` | Takes one column out as a plain vector | `pull(penguins, body_mass_g)` |
| `distinct()` | Lists each unique value once | `distinct(penguins, island)` |
| `pivot_longer()` | Turns wide column headings into tidy rows (from tidyr) | `pivot_longer(island_wide, cols = everything(), names_to = "island", values_to = "elev")` |
| `left_join()` | Adds columns from a second table by a shared key | `left_join(penguins, island_tidy, by = "island")` |
| <code>&#124;&gt;</code> | Passes the result of one step into the next | <code>penguins &#124;&gt; filter(species == &quot;Adelie&quot;) &#124;&gt; count(island)</code> |

Posit, the company that makes RStudio, publishes a one-page summary of all of these: [dplyr cheat sheet](https://rstudio.github.io/cheatsheets/html/data-transformation.html) (web page) or [PDF](https://rstudio.github.io/cheatsheets/data-transformation.pdf). Keep it open while you work on assignments.
:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Conditions: yes-or-no questions inside `filter()` (click to expand)

| Write | Meaning | Example inside `filter()` |
|---|---|---|
| `==` | is equal to | `species == "Adelie"` |
| `!=` | is not equal to | `species != "Gentoo"` |
| `>` and `>=` | greater than, greater than or equal to | `body_mass_g >= 4500` |
| `<` and `<=` | less than, less than or equal to | `flipper_length_mm < 190` |
| `%in%` | is one of these values | `species %in% c("Adelie", "Chinstrap")` |
| `is.na()` | is missing | `is.na(body_mass_g)` |
| `!` | not; flips TRUE and FALSE | `!is.na(body_mass_g)` |
| `&` or a comma | both must be true | `species == "Gentoo", body_mass_g >= 4500` |
| <code>&#124;</code> | either can be true | <code>island == &quot;Dream&quot; &#124; island == &quot;Biscoe&quot;</code> |

Text goes in quotes (`"Adelie"`); numbers do not (`4500`). One `=` assigns a value; two `==` compares. Each condition produces TRUE or FALSE for every row, and `filter()` keeps the TRUE rows.
:::

